00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.910
What I've done can
inform what you're

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:01.580
going to build going forward.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:06.400
And hopefully it will inform
what we'll build together.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.770
Number one, I built a proof
of concept using Dataflow that

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:15.830
took 10,000 30-year time
series of financial data

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:18.529
and correlated
everything by everything,

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:23.650
50 million correlations of
30 years' worth of data,

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:28.750
three minutes, at 300,000
correlations per second,

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:33.330
compared to running on a 16 core
server where I turned it off

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.710
after 12 hours
because it was just,

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.270
if it doesn't
finish in 12 hours,

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:40.580
that effectively sort of
goes towards infinity to me.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.700
It was very boring
to wait for it.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.590
I built a proof of concept
using Pub/Sub, Dataflow,

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:51.100
Cloud Storage, and Bigtable
that ingested a simulated market

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.390
data feed of all
North American market

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:59.000
data in graduations of a quarter
million events per second.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.560
Did that from Pub/Sub, and
I also did it as files.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.565
Took about two days
to code that up,

00:05:05.565 --> 00:05:09.430
and it took zero overhead in
setting it up and bringing it

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:10.380
down.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.790
Interestingly, I also
built Spark clusters

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.920
to do a grid search of
that data using Dataproc.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:21.730
Those clusters of 25
servers took less than a

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.870
minute to come up,
took about 45 seconds

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:28.550
based on my testing, which
means I was able to do that grid

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:32.820
search by instantiating
and then tearing down

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.670
whole clusters of machines.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:39.310
Finally and recently,
we built a machine

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:43.750
learning model that spans
Spark, ML, and TensorFlow to put

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:48.010
an ensemble together, to
see how those things worked.

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:50.210
And with that, I
wanted to hand it over

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.810
to Matt who's going to talk
about what Kensho's doing.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:54.650
Thank you very much.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.780
MATT TAYLOR: Thank you Corrie.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:06.950
Our partnership with Google on
GCP has been a real pleasure.

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.550
And I think one of the things
that attracted us to Google

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.480
