nature in which we

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.360
get to develop our products.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.140
He's also done a
fantastic job of kind

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:18.560
of showing off the raw power
that GCP provides to us.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.990
So today I'm going to
give you a personal look

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:23.270
under the hood of Kensho,
what our mission is,

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:24.842
how we think about the world.

00:06:24.842 --> 00:06:27.300
And most importantly, we'll
walk through a machine learning

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.640
experiment together, letting
the computer identify

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.170
what days are like this.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:36.900
So Kensho is a high-end
analytics platform

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.280
for the financial industry.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.700
Think machine learning powered
hedge fund analytics delivered

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:42.800
via the browser.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.140
If you work at a major
bank or a major hedge fund,

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.010
you're probably already
aware of our platform.

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:49.830
If you've watched
CNBC, you've likely

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:52.990
seen the Kensho stats box where
their anchors use our platform

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:55.210
to analyze events in the news.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.050
While we deliver hedge
fund caliber analytics,

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:01.210
we're not a hedge fund.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:02.584
And that's one of
the nice things

00:07:02.584 --> 00:07:04.580
that I can do today
is as a startup,

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:06.730
as a technology company,
I can come and give you

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.750
a peek under the hood of how
we treat financial analysis.

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:13.580
This is making sophisticated
financial analysis

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.740
more accessible,
intuitive, and beautiful.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.020
Now as part of the Kensho
team and a back-end data

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:23.270
nerd at heart, I'm passionate
about deleting code and using

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.650
technology to
understand the world.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:29.170
Machine learning accomplishes
both of these goals.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:31.790
I joined Kensho
in 2013, and I now

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:35.140
quarterback the engineering,
data science, and design teams.

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:37.480
And every day I rely
heavily on my experience

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.260
building low-level
financial infrastructure,

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.310
high-frequency trading, and
delivering millions of cat GIFs

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:45.330
at Tumblr.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:48.240
Kensho and our machine learning
on Google Cloud Platform

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.010
is the unity of these goals.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.790
Make it smart, make it
fast, and make it beautiful.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:08:00.070
Founded in 2013, Kensho,
like most three-year-olds,

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:01.510
we're curious about the world.

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:04.350
We come with few
preconceived notions.

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.690
We're eager to learn.

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:09.830
And we adapt quickly
to new information.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:13.320
We do this via search,
discovery, analysis, knowledge

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.060
graph, event database.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.320
We deal with single assets,
macro events, geopolitical,

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:19.090
weather.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:22.210
And today we're going
to ask the machine

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:23.690
to identify days like this.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.200
So like any good
experiment, we're

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.669
going to start with a
testable hypothesis.

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:34.580
We're going to plan
for our experiment.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.130
We're going to structure
a specific test.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:40.530
And we're going to let the
machine drive our analysis.

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.380
Then we're going to feed it
back into the Kensho platform

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:44.720
and see how the markets react.

00:08:50.957 --> 00:08:53.040
So if you've read a "Random
Walk Down Wall Street"

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:54.970
and believe an
efficient market theory,

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:58.010
you probably think
that the answer is no.

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:01.170
If you recall the financial
crisis and the Great Recession,

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:02.930
you might think
asset prices can be

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:06.600
grossly mispriced for
prolonged periods of time.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.280
You might think
the answer is yes.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:12.320
Using market events
and other features,

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:15.660
can I gain insight into
what may happen tomorrow?

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.380
Let's find out what
the computer thinks.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.120
In order to maximize
learning, there's

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.800
two things that we really need.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:30.040
And for hungry, lean companies,
the most important question

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:35.596
is, what is the most valuable
thing I can learn right now?

00:09:35.596 --> 00:09:41.220
And the two things I need are
resources and proper timing.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.120
So I want to take a
step back for a second

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.310
and talk about those
two things to make sure

00:09:45.310 --> 00:09:46.880
now is the time to act.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.720
Now for resources, I borrow a
lot from the same challenging

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.610
questions that face
our own education

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:57.460
system, about educating
our best and brightest

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:02.430
to keep America competitive
across a global marketplace.

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:05.070
You need physical safety
to explore and experience

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:06.740
what the world is around you.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:10.630
You need resources to fuel and
encourage and to grow with you.

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:14.140
You need a community
of mentors, guides,

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:16.700
and helping you
correct mistakes.

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:18.940
You need a culture
that encourages

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:23.190
you to learn from mistakes,
to try lots of things,

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:26.370
and to fail quickly.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:31.830
But above all, you have
to always be learning.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:34.170
And if we apply
this same approach

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:37.920
to how we teach our machines,
and then how our machines teach

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:43.380
us-- data center, security,
CPU, disk, the network

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:48.450
capacity, unparalleled
access to APIs, data,

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:52.210
the right permissions to
not do the wrong things,

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.260
we can do really powerful stuff.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.800
There becomes a tipping
point in the ecosystem

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.640
where it feeds back onto itself
and becomes a multiplier.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.990
Now the second thing we need
in addition to resources

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:05.280
was timing.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.520
And this is critical.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:09.037
And one of the
things that struck me

00:11:09.037 --> 00:11:10.870
was thinking about one
of my favorite things

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:13.940
in all the world, the
Large Hadron Collider.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:21.010
It took 10 years to build it--
10 years, from 1998 to 2008.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:23.720
It was a significant
international collaborative

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:25.230
effort by CERN.

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:28.330
It needed years of
theoretical testing

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:31.990
and smaller attempts to
build smaller colliders.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:37.140
It didn't have its first
test run until 2010.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:38.940
But it all paid off.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:43.130
In 2012, they discovered
the Higgs boson,

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:44.970
and is now being
used to measure some

00:11:44.970 --> 00:11:47.080
of the highest-energy
collisions in the world.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:54.260
I want to compare that to
what Kensho gets from GCP.

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:01.750
Instead of investing a
generation of knowledge

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:03.790
and science, and
instead of investing

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:06.800
a half a decade of
infrastructure build-out

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.240
and another decade to
actually get it running,

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:11.890
we spent minutes.

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:13.840
We measure our
scientific process

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.840
in minutes of time and money.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:21.270
Founded in 2013, we use
machine learning on GCP

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:23.780
to power our platform.

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:26.330
Now what struck me
at this is Google

00:12:26.330 --> 00:12:30.530
was founded in 1998 as well, the
same year they started building

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:33.200
the Large Hadron Collider.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.250
And while we can run countless
experiments every day

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:40.860
and scale up those experiments
to the largest in the world,

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:43.800
I asked myself, why
are we able to do this?

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.940
How can we go from a
generational planning

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:49.570
to doing science and
data science and research

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:52.590
and machine learning in minutes?

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.830
We're standing on top
of the other thing that

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:56.760
started in 1998.

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.560
Google has more than a decade
of building out infrastructure,

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:02.240
security, data expertise.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.190
And it struck me at
yesterday's keynote,

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:08.430
Diane Greene mentioned that
Google spends $9.9 billion

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.020
every year on capex.

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:13.970
And it cost approximately
$9.9 billion

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:17.690
to build the Large
Hadron Collider.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:21.610
So we are standing upon
the Large Hadron Collider

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:24.390
equivalent of cloud computing.

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:27.410
And that means we can start
signing customers, solving

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:29.700
our problems, and
doing machine learning

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:33.240
without having to build 17 miles
of tunnel underneath Europe.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.890
So if we have resources
and we have the timing

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:41.170
is right, because we can
do our experiments measured

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:44.280
in minutes, and we can do
those experiments in parallel

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:46.860
with everyone else who wants
to use this Large Hadron

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:48.970
Collider at the same time.

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:51.290
It's clear the time
to get started.

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.895
So let's move right away to
designing our experiment.

00:13:56.505 --> 00:13:58.380
So the first thing we're
going to do is we're

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:01.930
going to gather various
measures of sector performance--

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:07.260
their momentum, liquidity,
volatility, pricing, trends,

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:10.120
historical ranges of prices.

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:11.960
We're going to want to
normalize that data,

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:13.947
perhaps into log space.

00:14:13.947 --> 00:14:15.780
We're going to adjust
the ranges of the data

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:19.490
to prevent outliers from
squishing our data into buckets

00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:23.280
that prevent them from being
distinguished from each other.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.710
Then we're going to use
unsupervised machine learning

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:28.930
to identify key features
that have existed in market

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:31.140
days previous to this.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.600
Then we're going to use
unsupervised machine learning

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:36.060
to cluster those and
identify what days

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:38.474
are most similar to each other.

00:14:38.474 --> 00:14:40.140
And then we're going
to take this model,

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.920
feed it back into Kensho,
and we're going to ask,

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:44.860
what will happen the next day?

00:14:48.279 --> 00:14:49.570
So let's recall our hypothesis.

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:55.800
So now we're going
to take a moment

00:14:55.800 --> 00:14:58.760
to ask you to log out of
your retirement accounts.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:00.800
While machine learning
is amazingly powerful

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.520
and amazingly cool,
I'm an engineer, not

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.244
a financial advisor.

00:15:05.244 --> 00:15:06.910
Don't day trade your
retirement account.

00:15:10.199 --> 00:15:11.990
So the first thing I
said we're going to do

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:14.820
is we're going to gather
and normalize our data.

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:18.290
We do this in a compositional,
API-driven fashion

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:21.280
that is powered by
Google Cloud Platform.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:23.670
In this case, we
take a configuration

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:28.780
and we inject it with time
series information, event

00:15:28.780 --> 00:15:33.550
from our knowledge base and
our event almanac, measures

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:36.311
of price and momentum
and liquidity.

00:15:36.311 --> 00:15:38.810
And behind the scenes, we're
going to normalize and clean up

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:39.610
that data.

00:15:39.610 --> 00:15:42.630
I said this would just
be the good parts.

00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:45.190
This compositional style
of machine learning

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:46.870
means we can leverage
all that Google

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:51.360
has to offer in one notebook.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.670
In this case, I'm pulling
in almost 4,300 days

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:58.090
of market data with about
64 features for every day.

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.320
So we love matrices.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:07.590
And so do computers and so
does machine learning models.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:10.980
In this case, we're using
single value decomposition

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:13.270
to remove redundant data.

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:16.280
I'm not helping the
computer very much here.

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:19.000
This is the computer
telling me, hey,

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:21.910
I found some stuff you really
didn't need to include.

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:24.180
It's not that valuable.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:27.930
And in this case,
SVD says I can remove

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:31.247
a chunk of your information
and still keep almost 98%

00:16:31.247 --> 00:16:32.830
of the distinguishing
characteristics.

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:37.630
I'm asking the
computer literally

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:40.779
to say what can I throw away.

00:16:40.779 --> 00:16:42.320
The next thing that
we're going to do

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:45.150
is throw this into a
model called t-SNE.

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:48.070
In longer form, it's
t-distributed stochastic

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:49.900
neighbor embedding.

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:52.580
This reduces the
dimensionality of the data

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:55.840
from 64 dimensions down to two.

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:58.495
But it does not preserve
the state of the data.

00:16:58.495 --> 00:17:03.560
It is helpful for plotting it
in a human-understandable space.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:06.250
But I do want to emphasize, in
just a couple lines of code,

00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:08.579
I have told the
computer almost nothing.

00:17:08.579 --> 00:17:10.930
I'm challenging the
computer to tell me.

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:16.647
I'm going to continue
to be very lazy.

00:17:16.647 --> 00:17:18.980
I'm not going to pick or
estimate the number of clusters

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:20.970
that we should use beforehand.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:22.480
I'm not going to
make assumptions

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:24.950
about the internal
data structure.

00:17:24.950 --> 00:17:30.000
Affinity propagation essentially
says, what information

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:35.440
is passed from previous states
in the data to future states?

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:38.580
And the computer will
figure this out on its own.

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:41.990
As we layer these clusters
in two-dimensional space,

00:17:41.990 --> 00:17:44.380
the silhouette score
will give us essentially

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:47.830
what it sounds like it is--
an overlay of the data.

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:49.480
And to what extent
do the shadows

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:54.040
or silhouettes of those clusters
overlap or fit with each other?

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:57.430
Now a terrible fit would
say, I'm in a cluster,

00:17:57.430 --> 00:17:59.730
but I really belong
in some other cluster.

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:01.290
And a great fit
would say, I'm so

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:03.050
happy in my current
cluster I would never

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:04.190
want to be anywhere else.

00:18:04.190 --> 00:18:06.010
It's not even close.

00:18:06.010 --> 00:18:10.110
And the scores for a silhouette
range from negative 1 to 1.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:13.490
In this case we have a 0.15.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:14.702
We're doing OK.

00:18:14.702 --> 00:18:15.410
I've seen better.

00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:16.660
We can continue to improve it.

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:21.945
So let's visualize
this in two dimensions.

00:18:21.945 --> 00:18:23.850
So this is a pretty graph.

00:18:23.850 --> 00:18:27.560
There's some larger areas where
a lot of days appear similar.

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:30.190
There's some tighter
clusters that seem

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:32.335
to be really close together.

00:18:32.335 --> 00:18:36.000
Now Kensho deals so much
with historical precedents

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.250
that for me, I'm drawn
to the clusters that

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:40.330
have a lot of days.

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:43.170
This allows me to go back
and measure things that have

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:46.270
hundreds of days in similarity.

00:18:46.270 --> 00:18:49.840
But taking a moment, I've
already learned something here.

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:53.160
I can go pluck out clusters
that only have two in them

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:54.830
or three days in them.

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:56.560
And just the fact
that these days

00:18:56.560 --> 00:19:00.760
seem to be so distinguished
from all the others

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:02.906
can make them notable as well.

00:19:02.906 --> 00:19:06.750
If as the market day rolls on I
find the most similar days are

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:09.180
one of these tiny
clusters that seems to not

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:10.950
be similar to
anything else, that

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:14.700
can potentially be valuable
information all by itself.

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:17.310
So as the computer starts
organizing and sorting

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:19.880
your data and giving
you perspective

00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:23.210
on what is common
versus uncommon,

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:27.330
it gives you the power as a
human to make a judgment about,

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:28.540
is this rare?

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:30.580
And if it's rare, what do I do?

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:33.240
Or maybe it just sounded
rare, and now the computer

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:36.220
is telling me no, this
happens all the time.

00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:39.580
We ran into this with the
drop of the price of oil.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:44.310
We went from about
$140, dropped to $100.

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:47.770
And there was a big
discussion around that,

00:19:47.770 --> 00:19:52.410
because a $40 drop
in oil is enormous.

00:19:52.410 --> 00:19:53.980
But is it?

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.860
And we were able to
Kensho to show that it

00:19:56.860 --> 00:19:59.210
wasn't an unusual event.

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:01.410
It wasn't a buying opportunity.

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:04.560
There was room for it to drop,
because on a percentage basis,

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:08.090
oil dropping 20% or
30% was not uncommon.

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:13.420
And as the summer went on,
oil dropped to $70, to $60.

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:17.510
And more recently it
was down in the $30s.

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:19.970
So just the quantity
and how rare

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:22.290
something is is
valuable by itself.

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:27.000
So let's look at a specific day.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:31.380
And in this case, I
have chosen February 23,

00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.100
so about a month ago.

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:38.366
This particular cluster has
a couple hundred precedents

00:20:38.366 --> 00:20:39.440
in it.

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:41.550
And in this case I'm
looking at other dates.

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:43.550
And I've bucketed them by month.

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:45.930
In machine learning,
as the feeble human

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:47.920
who's participating
in this task,

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:50.580
I find histograms
immensely useful

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:52.890
because it gives me a sense
of the shape of the data,

00:20:52.890 --> 00:20:54.800
how it's laid out,
how the computer

00:20:54.800 --> 00:20:56.790
has organized it for me.

00:20:56.790 --> 00:20:59.810
And then I can look at this
and say, that's interesting.

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:05.060
So what's interesting about it?

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:10.550
Well, for one, I see there's a
lot more days like February 23

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:11.610
more recently.

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:15.930
There's a jump in 2014, 2015.

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:22.470
I don't see much in 2010, 2011,
2012, little spike in 2008,

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:24.600
not much in 2007.

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:27.660
And then I go back
into the early '00s.

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:29.950
I see there's more
clusters of data.

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:35.080
And intuitively, I
find this makes sense.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:38.460
The regime of the market
from the financial crisis

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:44.130
to about 2014 or early '15
was a long steady march

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:45.270
up to the right.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:47.660
It was recovering from
the Great Recession.

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:49.840
People's confidence
was going up.

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:54.260
The stock market was--
earnings were strong.

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:55.890
Revenue growth was strong.

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:57.640
The housing market
was showing signs

00:21:57.640 --> 00:21:59.660
of recovery and stabilization.

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:02.230
There was a quantitative
easing program

00:22:02.230 --> 00:22:05.310
going on that helped
fuel this progress.

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:08.880
And then as we approached
the 2015 window,

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:11.890
people started to think,
maybe the federal government

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:14.410
is going to start unwinding
quantitative easing.

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:16.120
People's confidence
started to rebound.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:17.995
People started to talk
a lot more about jobs.

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:24.170
So it says to me that perhaps
this machine has learned

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:27.420
something about the
structure of the market

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:29.200
and the overall
tone of the sectors

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:31.350
and how they behaved
with each other

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:35.130
as it changed from leading
into the financial crisis

00:22:35.130 --> 00:22:37.390
to the financial
crisis to recovery

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:39.160
from the financial
crisis to now.

00:22:41.435 --> 00:22:43.560
So what I want to do is I
want to take a moment now

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:46.610
to feed this into Kensho,
and we're going to ask,

00:22:46.610 --> 00:22:51.080
what happens on days
like February 23?

00:22:51.080 --> 00:22:52.620
Can we switch to the demo?

00:23:02.207 --> 00:23:03.290
So we're not a hedge fund.

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:04.310
We put out a website.

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:07.080
We sell our product to major
financial institutions.

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:09.290
And what I've done here
is extracted the dates

00:23:09.290 --> 00:23:11.510
that the machine has
told me are similar.

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:14.980
I have fed them into
our analysis engine.

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:18.460
In this case I have
256 previous dates.

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:20.240
Again, you can see
the histogram of how

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:22.240
they are clustered by days.

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:25.760
So I'm going to ask the system
a very specific question.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:31.830
If I buy on the close
of days like February 23

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:37.000
and I sell it the
next day, how do I do?

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:40.360
So I'm literally
taking, on February 23,

00:23:40.360 --> 00:23:41.820
I see we're coming
into the close.

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:45.690
The markets are open
from 9:30 to 4:00.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:48.030
Coming to a close, say
it's 3:30 in the day,

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:49.347
and I look at the stock prices.

00:23:49.347 --> 00:23:50.305
I look at my portfolio.

00:23:50.305 --> 00:23:52.490
I look at what's going
on in macro levels.

00:23:52.490 --> 00:23:54.510
I look at how the
sectors are performing.

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:56.360
I look at what's in the news.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:24:00.180
I run and I ask the machine,
what days are like today?

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:04.243
And the machine has told me, I
found more than 250 precedents.

00:24:04.243 --> 00:24:07.320
It's going to say, if I were
to buy-- if this ends up

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:09.920
being just like how I think
it's going to shape up

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:13.159
over the last half hour of
trading, what should I buy?

00:24:13.159 --> 00:24:14.700
Or if I'm already
holding it, maybe I

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:17.090
should sell, reduce my
risk, reduce my exposure.

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:24.370
In this case, I'm just going to
do some liquid ETFs, materials,

00:24:24.370 --> 00:24:30.390
energy, consumer discretionary,
financials, technology stocks.

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:34.170
The results, we can analyze what
happens on all of those dates.

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:38.230
We compute, on the fly, more
than 85 different statistics

00:24:38.230 --> 00:24:41.660
from beta-adjusted
returns to p-values

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:46.070
to your actual raw or
average daily returns.

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:49.990
We adjust for dividends,
corporate actions.

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:52.370
You can see the dispersion
in how the various market

00:24:52.370 --> 00:24:54.610
sectors pan out.

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:57.830
There's not tight clustering
around those events.

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:01.230
In this case, I'm looking
at the beta-adjusted return.

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:03.874
This helps normalize
the performance

00:25:03.874 --> 00:25:05.915
of the various sectors
relative to the benchmark.

00:25:05.915 --> 00:25:07.790
So I'm looking
for outperformance

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:11.210
or underperformance rather than
just maybe everything went up

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:11.960
on that given day.

00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:16.492
So what happens?

00:25:16.492 --> 00:25:19.790
So in this case, I'm going
to treat this returns

00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:22.790
chart as a stack rank.

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:24.800
And it seems like
in this case energy

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:28.200
has historically outperformed.

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:31.110
Materials is up there.

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:38.200
We have down at the bottom,
XLF is the financials.

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:41.020
I see health care
seems to underperform.

00:25:41.020 --> 00:25:42.770
So in this kind of
thing, I might say hey,

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:43.895
let me look at my holdings.

00:25:43.895 --> 00:25:46.130
Am I overweight health care?

00:25:46.130 --> 00:25:48.152
Should I do
something about that?

00:25:48.152 --> 00:25:49.610
If I was planning
on buying energy,

00:25:49.610 --> 00:25:54.160
should I buy it today
rather than tomorrow?

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:56.030
And lastly, we
give our customers

00:25:56.030 --> 00:25:57.670
all the raw data output.

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:01.300
We run tests, t-tests, both
one-sided and two-sided,

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:04.360
depending on the scenario
that you're trying to measure,

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:09.290
about looking for fat tail
risk and looking for shifts

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:12.020
in the distribution of returns.

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:13.740
We also look a lot
about time series.

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:17.210
So the way machine learning
teaches us about time series,

00:26:17.210 --> 00:26:19.420
we then feed that into
a visualization that

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:22.800
allows us to appreciate what
the machine has identified

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:26.860
and overlay that with how
the market has performed.

00:26:26.860 --> 00:26:31.770
So I'm going to say, sell
health care and buy some energy

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:35.890
or hedge my positions
on the 24th.

00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:37.920
So this allows me to
do something different.

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:41.040
So now I'm actually
asking, what happened?

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:43.160
I bought some
stocks on the close.

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:44.680
And now I held them.

00:26:44.680 --> 00:26:47.430
And here I am on the 24th
holding these stocks.

00:26:47.430 --> 00:26:49.115
So what happened
to my portfolio?

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:00.660
So I see materials
has done quite well.

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:03.450
I see energy is
still up at the top.

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:06.765
I see health care has
nudged a little bit.

00:27:06.765 --> 00:27:08.390
I'm not sure if
there's something here,

00:27:08.390 --> 00:27:11.110
but it does seem like the
top has generally stabilized.

00:27:11.110 --> 00:27:12.780
The bottom has
generally stabilized.

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:17.430
And there's some shuffling or
mixing around in the middle.

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:18.810
And it's this kind of thing.

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:20.720
I've told the computer
almost nothing other

00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:23.000
than here's some market data.

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:24.640
Remove what I don't need.

00:27:24.640 --> 00:27:26.160
Group it together.

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:27.900
Reduce the dimensionality.

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:30.880
Identify signals that are
passed from previous states

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:32.270
to future state.

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:35.260
And then fed that back into
an analysis engine to say,

00:27:35.260 --> 00:27:37.780
well, this looks like
this kind of day.

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:39.190
What happens tomorrow?

00:27:39.190 --> 00:27:44.110
And when I look at this, I
say this is not a random walk.

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:45.942
Can we go back to
my slides, please?

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:53.070
So let's bring
this all together.

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:56.520
We're really doing is
talking about understanding

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:58.780
the connections of things.

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:01.650
How are things
connected to each other?

00:28:01.650 --> 00:28:04.190
We're statistically annotating
the significance of what

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:05.980
has happened in the market.

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:08.730
So we're looking at
two market assets.

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:10.480
There's a major publicity event.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:11.910
A company has earnings.

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:15.060
There's unrest in
the Middle East.

00:28:15.060 --> 00:28:18.380
There's a new product announced
by a specific company.

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:21.190
To what extent does
that influence or alter

00:28:21.190 --> 00:28:26.040
how that asset performs in the
future, through market assets?

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:29.550
That means events happening in
the world, news happening now,

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:32.710
and to what extent does
that change our perception

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:36.060
of the likelihood or the
significance of events

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:38.190
coming in the future?

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:40.870
And then lastly, market
assets by themselves

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:43.120
can influence what
will happen as well.

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:46.437
And I'll go back again to
the drop in the price of oil,

00:28:46.437 --> 00:28:48.520
where you can see how the
drop in the price of oil

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:51.250
has caused ramifications
in the Middle East

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:53.410
around the structure
of those governments

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:55.220
and their ability
to pay their bills.

00:28:55.220 --> 00:28:57.240
It's affected
Alaska, which is now

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:00.060
having to start
considering income taxes

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:04.010
and change their budget
economics quite dramatically.

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:06.330
And ultimately through
machine learning,

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:12.130
we're answering the question,
who should care, and how much?

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:15.215
And if you decide that you
do or don't care, when?

00:29:15.215 --> 00:29:16.090
When should you care?

00:29:16.090 --> 00:29:17.070
When is it important?

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:21.710
When is the time to
act, and for how long?

00:29:21.710 --> 00:29:24.570
Now, I got a very interesting
question this morning

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:27.300
as I was watching the keynote.

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:30.360
And the person next to me
asked me about machine learning

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:32.985
and said, is this a
biological process

00:29:32.985 --> 00:29:36.990
or is this a
mathematical process?

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:40.330
I think that's a
fantastic question.

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:43.020
Because at the top
level, machine learning,

00:29:43.020 --> 00:29:45.510
neural networks
especially, they behave

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:47.710
like a biological process.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:49.120
There's layers to them.

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:50.130
There's nuance.

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:51.980
They incorporate new things.

00:29:51.980 --> 00:29:56.570
As new data comes in, you can
incrementally train your model

00:29:56.570 --> 00:30:00.740
in the same way that a child
discovering a toy or gravity

00:30:00.740 --> 00:30:03.860
or getting to visit the
Large Hadron Collider

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:07.110
incorporates new
information into its already

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:11.290
existing perception and
knowledge of the world.

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:14.435
But if you zoom into the
micro details of the model,

00:30:14.435 --> 00:30:17.740
you're trying to back out, does
this machine really get it?

00:30:17.740 --> 00:30:21.500
Do they really find
stuff that I can't see?

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:25.330
And you look at the individual
layers of the model,

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:27.202
it starts to look like math.

00:30:27.202 --> 00:30:29.070
It is math.

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:30.380
It's matrices.

00:30:30.380 --> 00:30:31.980
It's numbers.

00:30:31.980 --> 00:30:34.460
And you can peel apart
the layers of, say,

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:39.700
a neural network and see at
each layer, what does it know,

00:30:39.700 --> 00:30:43.640
and how does this change what
it knew from the previous layer,

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:45.960
in the same way we might
test a child in school

00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:48.386
to say, what did
you learn this year,

00:30:48.386 --> 00:30:50.510
and compare that to how
they did the previous year.

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:55.820
And this is our brains as well.

00:30:55.820 --> 00:30:58.870
We think of our brains
as a biological process,

00:30:58.870 --> 00:31:00.550
because they are.

00:31:00.550 --> 00:31:02.230
It's lots of connections.

00:31:02.230 --> 00:31:03.170
It's flaws.

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:04.130
We learn something.

00:31:04.130 --> 00:31:07.560
We unlearn it later.

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:10.349
But then you zoom
into the micro detail,

00:31:10.349 --> 00:31:11.765
and our brains are
firing neurons.

00:31:11.765 --> 00:31:12.945
It's ones and zeros.

00:31:12.945 --> 00:31:16.350
Did it fire or not?

00:31:16.350 --> 00:31:18.000
And so when I look
at how we analyze

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:21.330
the connectedness of things
in the world, the increasing

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:24.180
velocity at which
information comes in,

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:26.800
we rely increasingly
heavily on the computers

00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:29.710
to process this information,
like the prodigy

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:33.200
that it is, at a velocity
that I can't match.

00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:35.150
And it tells me things.

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:37.840
And that allows me to
delete code, replace it

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:40.150
with models that
learn and update,

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:43.637
and then go back to solving
my customer's problem.

00:31:43.637 --> 00:31:44.470
Thank you very much.

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:54.431
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:54.431 --> 00:31:55.430
I can do some questions.

00:31:55.430 --> 00:31:57.304
I have about 10
minutes if people

00:31:57.304 --> 00:31:58.470
would like to ask questions.

00:32:03.310 --> 00:32:03.810
Yes?

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:09.156
AUDIENCE: If we're
deciding if today

00:32:09.156 --> 00:32:12.719
is similar to other days in the
past, what data did you use?

00:32:12.719 --> 00:32:14.260
MATT TAYLOR: So the
question is, what

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:18.380
data did I use to decide
what was similar in the past?

00:32:18.380 --> 00:32:22.070
In this particular model, I
was using pricing, volume,

00:32:22.070 --> 00:32:29.220
volatility, ranges, moving
averages, various ETFs

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:31.190
representing various sectors.

00:32:31.190 --> 00:32:33.400
There's a lot more stuff
that you can pull in

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:37.330
and layer increasing nuance
on top of these things.

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:39.830
For example, one
thing we frequently do

00:32:39.830 --> 00:32:43.140
is we'll pull in when earnings
and other major shock level

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:45.590
events will affect an asset.

00:32:45.590 --> 00:32:49.070
The volume of ETFs has
increased and varied over years,

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:50.930
so you probably
want an ARIMA model

00:32:50.930 --> 00:32:53.200
that normalizes some
of the seasonality out

00:32:53.200 --> 00:32:53.930
of those things.

00:32:57.520 --> 00:32:59.820
I probably could have
picked additional sectors,

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:01.920
or even want to study
something at a macro level.

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:03.836
Rather in this case I
was looking at something

00:33:03.836 --> 00:33:06.350
that was very US centric.

00:33:06.350 --> 00:33:09.170
So one of the things we find
very valuable about being

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:11.680
able to run rapid
experiments in the cloud

00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:13.090
is you want to
pull in more data,

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:15.460
we can scale up the
computers to do that.

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:18.040
If the experiment seems
not to be fruitful,

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:18.970
we throw that away.

00:33:18.970 --> 00:33:21.400
We didn't have to build
out a huge infrastructure

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:24.024
and then scurry around trying to
get teams to make sure they're

00:33:24.024 --> 00:33:25.250
always utilizing it.

00:33:25.250 --> 00:33:29.710
The second part is it allows
us to study sample sets.

00:33:29.710 --> 00:33:32.070
And if it looks promising,
then we scale up the project

00:33:32.070 --> 00:33:33.570
accordingly.

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:36.040
So I think the big [INAUDIBLE]
is, the compositional style

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:38.490
allows us to inject different
features and different data

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:39.540
and play with that.

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:43.160
And then when we find something,
we can iterate on that.

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:45.850
Question?

00:33:45.850 --> 00:33:48.740
AUDIENCE: Have you
tried feeding this model

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:51.230
with historical
data, but omitting

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:55.450
some of the significant days,
and see if your model could

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:56.389
have predicted those?

00:33:56.389 --> 00:33:57.180
CORRIE ELSTON: Yes.

00:33:57.180 --> 00:34:01.000
So the question is,
can you essentially do

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:03.220
an in-sample, out-sample test.

00:34:03.220 --> 00:34:08.250
So you take, say, 80%
or 85% of your days.

00:34:08.250 --> 00:34:09.770
You train on that data.

00:34:09.770 --> 00:34:13.320
And then you use it as an out
of sample test on the other 15%

00:34:13.320 --> 00:34:16.290
or 20% of the data.

00:34:16.290 --> 00:34:19.159
In this case, I think you'd want
to pull in additional features

00:34:19.159 --> 00:34:21.219
in order to do that
well, so please

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:22.560
don't trade the strategy.

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:26.046
But at the same time, that's
exactly the right thing to do.

00:34:26.046 --> 00:34:28.420
And the other nice thing,
because we have this complement

00:34:28.420 --> 00:34:30.960
of machine learning and
back-testing analysis,

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:32.560
we're able to
check those things.

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:35.260
And then the incremental
layering of machine learning

00:34:35.260 --> 00:34:37.564
allows you to constantly
incorporate new information.

00:34:37.564 --> 00:34:39.449
It would be like, what
did I miss yesterday?

00:34:39.449 --> 00:34:40.500
Let me incorporate that.

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:41.460
What did I get right?

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:43.370
Let me reinforce that.

00:34:43.370 --> 00:34:46.100
And so the model of learning
is very much fail quickly,

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:46.860
do again.

00:34:46.860 --> 00:34:52.100
But machines, unlike maybe
humans, don't develop a bias.

00:34:52.100 --> 00:34:55.000
Our feelings don't get hurt
if you're constantly wrong.

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:57.080
Computers don't get discouraged.

00:34:57.080 --> 00:34:59.150
And so then their velocity
of running experiments

00:34:59.150 --> 00:35:01.780
so fast is such a
multiplier that they're

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:05.860
able to fail so many times until
they get it right, at a scale

00:35:05.860 --> 00:35:09.140
that none of us can match.

00:35:09.140 --> 00:35:09.640
Michael?

00:35:09.640 --> 00:35:12.262
AUDIENCE: So did it
predict February 24?

00:35:12.262 --> 00:35:14.220
CORRIE ELSTON: I think
it got some things right

00:35:14.220 --> 00:35:17.750
and some things wrong, in that
I think the general-- if you

00:35:17.750 --> 00:35:20.030
think of it as like
layers of sediment,

00:35:20.030 --> 00:35:23.640
the things that were generally
doing well historically

00:35:23.640 --> 00:35:25.860
were generally toward
the top, and things

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:29.640
that were doing poorly were
generally toward the bottom.

00:35:29.640 --> 00:35:31.390
But I think you'd
again want to-- I

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:34.160
wouldn't go trade your
retirement account based

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:35.015
on that.

00:35:35.015 --> 00:35:37.220
And I think there's more
you could layer in there.

00:35:37.220 --> 00:35:39.200
But yeah, I think
in general it seemed

00:35:39.200 --> 00:35:40.460
to have some sorting there.

00:35:40.460 --> 00:35:43.270
And a sophisticated
financial investor

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:46.440
would probably look at some
sort of weighted strategy

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:48.400
that would buy some
things at a higher

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:52.180
ratio or short other things or
sell things in a various ratio

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:56.280
to potentially hedge or
leverage opportunity.

00:35:56.280 --> 00:35:57.756
AUDIENCE: Did it
identify any days

00:35:57.756 --> 00:35:59.522
that had never happened before?

00:35:59.522 --> 00:36:01.230
CORRIE ELSTON: So the
question is, did it

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:05.560
identify any days that
had never happened before?

00:36:05.560 --> 00:36:08.090
So there was a bunch.

00:36:08.090 --> 00:36:10.210
So we have a precedent
engine, right?

00:36:10.210 --> 00:36:12.850
So we use machine
learning to identify

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:16.310
precedents of when something
has happened like this.

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:17.930
And if there aren't
many precedents,

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:21.070
then you really can't run a test
on what has happened before,

00:36:21.070 --> 00:36:23.660
although it may be valuable
to know it hasn't happened.

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:25.060
So when there are
small clusters,

00:36:25.060 --> 00:36:28.430
I just basically threw
out ones that had,

00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:31.240
I think, it was just a
handful of days in them,

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:34.670
because I don't have a
robust statistical way

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:38.240
of feeding those back in
to do analysis on those.

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:41.530
But certainly, say, if
I had a dashboard that

00:36:41.530 --> 00:36:44.180
tells me going into
the close, hey,

00:36:44.180 --> 00:36:46.860
this looks kind of unprecedented
because of these reasons

00:36:46.860 --> 00:36:50.850
and why, that's something that,
at the scale of the information

00:36:50.850 --> 00:36:55.890
coming into the system,
no individual could do.

00:36:55.890 --> 00:36:56.950
Microphone, yes?

00:36:56.950 --> 00:37:00.850
AUDIENCE: So the
Python code you showed

00:37:00.850 --> 00:37:03.000
is written inside
a browser, and that

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:05.990
would trigger your
machine learning library

00:37:05.990 --> 00:37:08.872
on Google Cloud Platform?

00:37:08.872 --> 00:37:10.330
CORRIE ELSTON: Yes,
so the question

00:37:10.330 --> 00:37:15.770
being-- the question's
about coding in the browser,

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:17.760
using an IPython notebook.

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:19.390
So we love APIs.

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:20.350
We love services.

00:37:20.350 --> 00:37:22.447
We love compositional services.

00:37:22.447 --> 00:37:24.780
So one of the things we do
is we build our own services,

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:25.405
our own models.

00:37:25.405 --> 00:37:28.940
We use Google's services
and Google's APIs.

00:37:28.940 --> 00:37:30.650
And then that allows
our data scientists

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:32.300
to work in an IPython notebook.

00:37:32.300 --> 00:37:36.320
And that essentially serves
as a journal of what this is.

00:37:36.320 --> 00:37:38.860
And they can pull things in.

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:40.660
And if the experiment
doesn't work out,

00:37:40.660 --> 00:37:42.840
we can stash that in
a Git repo and someone

00:37:42.840 --> 00:37:47.440
can pick it up later or
at least learn from that.

00:37:47.440 --> 00:37:49.440
And so the way we do
this , we have an API--

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:52.940
I didn't show it here-- that
we can kick off analysis

00:37:52.940 --> 00:37:56.990
in the background and
pull those back in.

00:37:56.990 --> 00:38:01.170
AUDIENCE: So instead of Google's
TensorFlow-based machine

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:05.050
learning framework, you
manage your own machine

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:06.720
learning models and libraries?

00:38:06.720 --> 00:38:07.803
CORRIE ELSTON: We do both.

00:38:09.970 --> 00:38:12.895
I got just a couple
more minutes.

00:38:12.895 --> 00:38:13.395
Right here?

00:38:13.395 --> 00:38:15.060
AUDIENCE: Have you
applied your model

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:17.393
to other data sets-- I'm just
curious-- as an experiment

00:38:17.393 --> 00:38:19.720
other than financials?

00:38:19.720 --> 00:38:22.150
CORRIE ELSTON: Yes.

00:38:22.150 --> 00:38:24.110
We've certainly
played around with it.

00:38:24.110 --> 00:38:26.280
We're very much focused
on financial information

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:27.440
right now.

00:38:27.440 --> 00:38:31.825
The financial industry is
heavily focused on security.

00:38:31.825 --> 00:38:34.470
They have very high
standards for robustness,

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:38.740
throughput, the large scale
data which keeps it interesting.

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:41.250
There's clients, there's an
immediate hunger, like answer

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:41.880
now.

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:43.920
And I think that
has a lot of analogs

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:46.760
across many different
sectors and data sets.

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:48.670
We ingest a large
variety of data.

00:38:48.670 --> 00:38:52.000
So it's not just numbers
from market data,

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:54.010
but we also do
world events, news.

00:38:54.010 --> 00:38:56.110
We have unstructured
data that we

00:38:56.110 --> 00:39:00.150
parse out and do
entity identification

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:02.470
and to kind of tease
out what's connected.

00:39:02.470 --> 00:39:04.860
We feed a lot of that
into our knowledge graph

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:09.220
so that we can understand
the connectedness of things,

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:11.810
like proverbial, you could
shake the knowledge graph

00:39:11.810 --> 00:39:14.070
and see what else
shakes, and for how much,

00:39:14.070 --> 00:39:16.170
and over what amount of time.

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:21.365
And our knowledge graph has
object-oriented structure to it

00:39:21.365 --> 00:39:22.890
that allows us to
validate things.

00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:24.889
And a lot of those
annotations are time-related,

00:39:24.889 --> 00:39:27.110
because we love mapping
things to time series,

00:39:27.110 --> 00:39:32.310
allows us to throw into matrices
and do machine learning on it.

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:32.810
Microphone.

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:40.640
AUDIENCE: When you're talking
about doing machine learning,

00:39:40.640 --> 00:39:42.800
you're working with
historical data.

00:39:42.800 --> 00:39:46.400
But in the past, there
were no other companies

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:49.520
doing machine learning on data
in the early 2000s, 1990s.

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:52.810
So how do you say that
your model is valid

00:39:52.810 --> 00:39:56.740
compared to historical data when
all financial institutions are

00:39:56.740 --> 00:39:59.390
doing this kind of analysis now?

00:39:59.390 --> 00:40:02.322
CORRIE ELSTON: Sure,
so the question

00:40:02.322 --> 00:40:04.030
being that lots of
people are doing this.

00:40:04.030 --> 00:40:05.820
And I think it's true.

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:09.040
For example, Google's been doing
this for quite a long time.

00:40:09.040 --> 00:40:10.860
And I think a big chunk
of machine learning

00:40:10.860 --> 00:40:13.770
is knowing what to
pay attention to

00:40:13.770 --> 00:40:15.760
and letting the
computer advise that.

00:40:15.760 --> 00:40:18.080
And I think a lot of what's
going to revolutionize

00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:20.977
the finance industry and
many other industries as well

00:40:20.977 --> 00:40:22.560
is they're going to
move to the cloud.

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:24.470
They're going to use machine
learning to remove code.

00:40:24.470 --> 00:40:26.720
They're going to use machine
learning to analyze data.

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:29.500
They're going to do it
in near to real time.

00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:31.470
And then the humans
will be enlightened

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:33.180
as a result of that.

00:40:33.180 --> 00:40:36.330
So it flips the
teacher-student relationship.

00:40:36.330 --> 00:40:39.070
As an engineer, I'm so used
to basically spelling out

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:40.760
a recipe for the computer.

00:40:40.760 --> 00:40:45.850
Do A, then B, then C, or call
A and then call B on that

00:40:45.850 --> 00:40:48.170
or call C on that.

00:40:48.170 --> 00:40:49.520
And this flips the relationship.

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:51.180
So now it's rather
than a couple people

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:52.980
trying to teach a
lot of machines,

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:54.570
we basically say
here's the framework

00:40:54.570 --> 00:40:57.390
and data for the
machines to learn,

00:40:57.390 --> 00:40:59.420
and then they will teach us.

00:40:59.420 --> 00:41:02.310
And that I think is an immensely
powerful paradigm switch

00:41:02.310 --> 00:41:03.390
that flips everything.

00:41:06.700 --> 00:41:09.230
There was another
question back there?

00:41:09.230 --> 00:41:12.010
I got-- last one.

00:41:12.010 --> 00:41:14.020
AUDIENCE: I have been
playing with Watson.

00:41:14.020 --> 00:41:17.090
And the ability to
actually type in a question

00:41:17.090 --> 00:41:19.274
and then it understands
the semantics of what

00:41:19.274 --> 00:41:21.440
you are asking or the context
of what you're asking,

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:22.180
it gives you an answer.

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:24.220
Have you built any of
that into your product?

00:41:24.220 --> 00:41:27.520
And have you leveraged any
of Google's machine learning

00:41:27.520 --> 00:41:28.950
algorithms to do that?

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:33.614
CORRIE ELSTON: Yes, so we love
natural language processing.

00:41:33.614 --> 00:41:35.030
I think it's a
very intuitive way.

00:41:35.030 --> 00:41:38.660
Search is a very
powerful tool for us

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:41.410
and for the financial industry.

00:41:41.410 --> 00:41:43.520
We have a query engine
where you can type

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:46.490
in more free-form questions.

00:41:46.490 --> 00:41:48.770
Where I think it becomes
exponentially powerful

00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:52.110
is I shouldn't have
to ask the computer.

00:41:52.110 --> 00:41:54.400
So we're evolving
from a world that

00:41:54.400 --> 00:41:56.490
says, computer what about
this, what about this,

00:41:56.490 --> 00:41:58.932
what about this, and it
behaves like hunt and peck.

00:41:58.932 --> 00:42:01.390
And we're coming to a world
where we say no, like computer,

00:42:01.390 --> 00:42:03.980
just let me know when it's
important, when I should care,

00:42:03.980 --> 00:42:05.890
for how much and how long.

00:42:05.890 --> 00:42:07.890
And so I think they both
play off to each other.

00:42:07.890 --> 00:42:10.210
The other area where
we use a lot of NLP

00:42:10.210 --> 00:42:13.300
is on the ingestion
side to identify things.

00:42:13.300 --> 00:42:15.520
And you can use
hidden Markov models

00:42:15.520 --> 00:42:18.030
to pull out structure
and implications

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:21.680
without even necessarily
needing entity identification.

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:23.680
So there's a lot of cool
stuff coming out there.

00:42:23.680 --> 00:42:26.110
And Google's a major
leader in that area,

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:29.308
especially on the tech side.

00:42:29.308 --> 00:42:31.364
All right, last one.

00:42:31.364 --> 00:42:35.720
AUDIENCE: Can you extend
the point on the analysis

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:38.140
for do you sell [INAUDIBLE]?

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:40.560
Let's say natural
language processing

00:42:40.560 --> 00:42:42.120
before you could
put it in anything.

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:43.620
CORRIE ELSTON: Yeah,
so the question

00:42:43.620 --> 00:42:47.280
is, do we sell the final
product or do we perhaps sell

00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:48.290
pieces of it?

00:42:48.290 --> 00:42:49.870
The answer is we do both.

00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:51.450
So we're building
a platform that

00:42:51.450 --> 00:42:53.810
is very flexible, has
many layers to it we

00:42:53.810 --> 00:42:55.430
can scale separately.

00:42:55.430 --> 00:42:59.500
We follow the Google GCP
model of microservices

00:42:59.500 --> 00:43:01.140
and being able to
expose those to have

00:43:01.140 --> 00:43:02.770
the right layer of abstraction.

00:43:02.770 --> 00:43:05.924
And we potentially open those
up as they become ready.

00:43:05.924 --> 00:43:07.024
Cool.

00:43:07.024 --> 00:43:08.190
Thank you so much, everyone.

00:43:08.190 --> 00:43:11.840
[APPLAUSE]

